- How would you implement testing for the planner with unreliable S3 access?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical RAG implementation skills and understanding of how chunking affects retrieval quality - critical for SAP's document-heavy use cases.

## Expected Answer
Strong candidates mention: 1) Hierarchical chunking respecting document structure, 2) Token overlap (200-300 tokens) to preserve context, 3) Variable chunk sizes based on content density, 4) Metadata preservation (section titles, page numbers), 5) Testing different chunk sizes for optimal retrieval.

## Code Example
```typescript
function chunkDocument(text: string, maxTokens: number = 500) {
  const sections = text.split(/\n(?=#{1,3}\s)/); // Split by headers
  const chunks = [];

  for (const section of sections) {
    const sentences = section.split('. ');
    let currentChunk = '';

    for (let i = 0; i < sentences.length; i++) {
      const testChunk = currentChunk + sentences[i] + '. ';
      if (countTokens(testChunk) > maxTokens) {
        chunks.push(currentChunk.trim());
        currentChunk = sentences[i - 2] + '. ' + sentences[i - 1] + '. '; // Overlap
      } else {
        currentChunk = testChunk;
      }
    }
    chunks.push(currentChunk.trim());
  }
  return chunks;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle code blocks or tables in technical documents?
- What metrics would you use to evaluate chunking effectiveness?
- How does chunk size impact embedding quality and retrieval speed?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests system design skills for production AI systems, understanding of trade-offs between accuracy and latency, and knowledge of evaluation metrics in real-world scenarios.

## Expected Answer
Strong candidates will discuss: 1) Multi-stage evaluation architecture, 2) Confidence threshold tuning, 3) Fallback strategies, 4) Monitoring and alerting, 5) Cost optimization through early filtering.

## Code Example
```typescript
class HallucinationDetector {
  async evaluate(response: string, context: string) {
    const semanticScore = await this.semanticCheck(response, context);
    if (semanticScore > 0.9) return { confident: true, score: semanticScore };

    const factualScore = await this.factualVerification(response);
    const ensembleScore = this.ensembleWeighting(semanticScore, factualScore);

    return {
      confident: ensembleScore > 0.85,
      score: ensembleScore,
      needsReview: ensembleScore < 0.7
    };
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle edge cases where the knowledge graph is incomplete?
- What metrics would you track to optimize the confidence thresholds?
- How would you design A/B testing for new detection algorithms?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of on-device, privacy-preserving ML systems with per-domain adapters that load dynamically while maintaining strict privacy guarantees in a chat application context.

## Key Concepts
- On-device fine-tuning with per-domain adapters
- Dynamic adapter loading from secure knowledge bases
- Adapter injection points (attention vs FFN layers)
- Quantization trade-offs (4-bit vs 8-bit)
- Domain gating and routing strategies
- Memory budgeting for mobile deployment
- Cross-domain evaluation and privacy compliance

## Code Example
```javascript
// Adapter hub loads domain-specific adapters dynamically
class AdapterHub {
  async loadAdapter(domain) {
    const adapter = await this.secureKB.fetch(domain);
    this.activeAdapters.set(domain, adapter);
  }

  route(query) {
    return this.gatingModel.predict(query);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to architect heterogeneous retrieval with dynamic chunking, versioned embeddings, and streaming results. It also probes freshness, updates, and cross-doc weighting in a unified vector store.

## Key Concepts

- Heterogeneous doc types
- Per-type dynamic chunking
- Versioned embeddings and metadata
- Cross-document re-ranking
- Streaming partial results

## Code Example

```javascript
// Skeleton: per-type chunk sizes and basic chunking hook
const CHUNK_SPECS = { api: 512, policy: 1024, incident: 1024 };
function chunkDocument(doc){
  const max = CHUNK_SPECS[doc.type] ?? 512;
  // simple approach: split into sentences then group by max tokens
  const sentences = splitIntoSentences(doc.text);
  const chunks = [];
  let cur = '';
  for (const s of sentences){
    if ((cur + s).length > max){ chunks.push(cur.trim()); cur = s; }
    else { cur += s; }
  }
  if (cur) chunks.push(cur.trim());
  return chunks;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle updates to docs and avoid stale embeddings?
- What metrics and tests validate per-type chunking efficacy and latency?
